# TCP vs UDP: Key Differences and When to Use Each

## Core Differences

| Aspect | TCP | UDP |
|--------|-----|-----|
| **Connection** | Connection-oriented (3-way handshake) | Connectionless |
| **Reliability** | Guaranteed delivery, ordering, and integrity | Best-effort; no guarantees |
| **Error handling** | Retransmits lost packets, checksums | Checksums only; drops bad packets |
| **Ordering** | Packets reassembled in order | No ordering guarantee |
| **Flow/Congestion control** | Yes (windowing, slow start, etc.) | None |
| **Header size** | 20–60 bytes | 8 bytes |
| **Speed/Overhead** | Slower, higher overhead | Faster, minimal overhead |
| **Broadcast/Multicast** | Not supported | Supported |
| **Data model** | Byte stream | Datagrams (message boundaries preserved) |

## How They Work

**TCP** establishes a connection via a three-way handshake (SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK), numbers every byte, acknowledges receipt, retransmits lost segments, and adjusts its sending rate based on network conditions. This makes it behave like a reliable pipe.

**UDP** just fires datagrams at the destination. Each packet is independent — no handshake, no acknowledgments, no retransmission. If a packet is lost or arrives out
